WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.415 --> 00:00:07.846
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.848 --> 00:00:10.202
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.203 --> 00:00:12.603
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.605 --> 00:00:14.937
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.937 --> 00:00:17.392
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.394 --> 00:00:19.648
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.649 --> 00:00:21.389
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.734 --> 00:00:24.115
I'm Alastair Budge, and today

00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:26.825
it's time for the third and final

00:00:26.828 --> 00:00:28.977
part of our mini-series on three

00:00:28.979 --> 00:00:31.260
cities that shaped modern Britain.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:34.386
In part one, we saw how Edinburgh

00:00:34.388 --> 00:00:36.915
lost its parliament and became the

00:00:36.962 --> 00:00:39.019
intellectual capital of the world.

00:00:39.368 --> 00:00:41.964
In part two, we watched Manchester

00:00:41.966 --> 00:00:44.636
invent the industrial city, and the

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.996
movements that tried to tame it.

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.976
And today, we finish in the south-west

00:00:49.978 --> 00:00:52.613
of England, in Bristol, the city that

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:55.493
opened England's door to the Atlantic,

00:00:55.495 --> 00:00:58.177
grew rich on the slave trade, and is

00:00:58.181 --> 00:01:01.086
still arguing about that history today.

00:01:02.056 --> 00:01:04.199
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:04.201 --> 00:01:06.590
minute and get right into it.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.119
It is the 7th of June, 2020, a Sunday

00:01:11.121 --> 00:01:14.117
afternoon in the centre of Bristol.

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:18.280
Thousands of people have marched through

00:01:18.282 --> 00:01:21.229
the city in support of the Black Lives

00:01:21.255 --> 00:01:24.032
Matter movement, two weeks after the

00:01:24.034 --> 00:01:26.732
killing of George Floyd in America.

00:01:28.084 --> 00:01:31.028
The march arrives at a bronze statue

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.808
of a man in old-fashioned clothes,

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:36.382
standing on a stone pedestal.

00:01:37.416 --> 00:01:40.329
He has stood there, looking down

00:01:40.331 --> 00:01:43.151
on the city centre, since 1895.

00:01:44.486 --> 00:01:45.574
Ropes appear.

00:01:45.965 --> 00:01:48.948
They are looped around the statue's head.

00:01:49.619 --> 00:01:52.396
And within about thirty seconds,

00:01:52.398 --> 00:01:54.912
a figure that has stood for a

00:01:54.923 --> 00:01:57.715
hundred and twenty-five years comes

00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:00.267
crashing down onto the pavement.

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:03.628
One protester kneels on the

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.139
statue's bronze neck for eight

00:02:06.199 --> 00:02:08.725
minutes and forty-six seconds, the

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:11.177
length of time that, according to

00:02:11.179 --> 00:02:13.879
the reports of the day, a police

00:02:13.881 --> 00:02:16.747
officer had knelt on George Floyd.

00:02:17.778 --> 00:02:21.048
Then the crowd rolls the statue through

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.982
the streets, hundreds of metres, to

00:02:23.982 --> 00:02:27.297
the edge of the old harbour, and tips

00:02:27.299 --> 00:02:30.433
it over the railing into the water.

00:02:31.741 --> 00:02:33.939
The statue was of a man

00:02:33.941 --> 00:02:35.945
named Edward Colston.

00:02:37.276 --> 00:02:39.326
Pictures of the splash went

00:02:39.328 --> 00:02:41.603
around the world within hours.

00:02:42.301 --> 00:02:45.410
Some people cheered; some were horrified.

00:02:46.211 --> 00:02:48.001
Everyone, suddenly, was

00:02:48.003 --> 00:02:49.714
talking about Bristol.

00:02:50.472 --> 00:02:53.175
To understand how a bronze statue of

00:02:53.177 --> 00:02:56.029
a merchant ended up at the bottom of a

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.242
harbour, we need to go back more than five

00:02:59.244 --> 00:03:02.272
hundred years, to the time when the city

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:05.249
first turned its face towards the ocean.

00:03:06.359 --> 00:03:09.390
Bristol, as you may know, is in the

00:03:09.392 --> 00:03:12.162
south-west of England, about 200

00:03:12.201 --> 00:03:15.031
kilometres west of London and a short

00:03:15.033 --> 00:03:17.556
drive from the border with Wales.

00:03:18.555 --> 00:03:21.127
Today it's a city of around half a

00:03:21.129 --> 00:03:24.001
million people, with a long reputation

00:03:24.362 --> 00:03:26.408
for doing things its own way.

00:03:27.844 --> 00:03:30.503
It grew up around a sheltered river

00:03:30.505 --> 00:03:33.238
harbour, a few miles inland from the

00:03:33.238 --> 00:03:35.688
point where the River Avon empties

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.498
into the sea between England and Wales.

00:03:40.005 --> 00:03:41.922
By the Middle Ages, it was one

00:03:41.924 --> 00:03:43.966
of the largest towns in England,

00:03:44.491 --> 00:03:47.125
third only to London and York.

00:03:48.265 --> 00:03:50.165
Its business was trade:

00:03:50.744 --> 00:03:52.344
woollen cloth going

00:03:52.346 --> 00:03:54.365
out, and wine coming in.

00:03:55.555 --> 00:03:57.155
Its ships sailed to

00:03:57.157 --> 00:03:59.514
Ireland, Spain and Portugal.

00:04:00.362 --> 00:04:02.859
Bristol's merchants made their money

00:04:02.861 --> 00:04:05.494
from the sea, and its sailors knew the

00:04:05.494 --> 00:04:07.950
waters leading out into the Atlantic.

00:04:09.001 --> 00:04:12.252
And in the 1490s, a Venetian

00:04:12.254 --> 00:04:15.968
visitor arrived with a proposal.

00:04:17.119 --> 00:04:20.032
Instead of following the familiar

00:04:20.034 --> 00:04:23.386
trading routes, he wanted to sail west

00:04:23.435 --> 00:04:27.245
across the ocean, hoping to reach Asia.

00:04:28.575 --> 00:04:30.736
His name was Zuan Caboto.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.644
The English knew him as John Cabot.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.647
He had persuaded King Henry VII to give

00:04:38.649 --> 00:04:41.295
him permission to attempt this voyage.

00:04:42.138 --> 00:04:44.458
The king, however, provided

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.349
no ships and no money.

00:04:47.515 --> 00:04:49.932
Cabot needed to find those in

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:52.433
Bristol, and find them he did.

00:04:53.608 --> 00:04:57.003
On the 2nd of May 1497, he sailed

00:04:57.005 --> 00:05:00.398
out of Bristol harbour in a small

00:05:00.516 --> 00:05:02.914
wooden ship called the Matthew,

00:05:02.916 --> 00:05:05.621
with a crew of around eighteen men.

00:05:06.765 --> 00:05:09.157
They sailed due west, and

00:05:09.159 --> 00:05:12.027
seven weeks later, on the 24th

00:05:12.027 --> 00:05:14.366
of June, they sighted land.

00:05:15.192 --> 00:05:17.027
It wasn't Asia, of course; it

00:05:17.029 --> 00:05:19.116
was the east coast of what is now

00:05:19.146 --> 00:05:21.599
Canada, probably Newfoundland.

00:05:22.996 --> 00:05:25.332
It was the first recorded European

00:05:25.334 --> 00:05:28.011
landing in mainland North America since

00:05:28.016 --> 00:05:30.637
the Vikings, five hundred years before.

00:05:31.992 --> 00:05:35.129
Back in England, the king gave Cabot a

00:05:35.131 --> 00:05:38.184
reward of ten pounds, plus a pension.

00:05:39.066 --> 00:05:40.967
The royal account book records

00:05:40.969 --> 00:05:43.122
it, rather wonderfully, as payment

00:05:43.423 --> 00:05:45.521
"to him that found the new isle".

00:05:46.823 --> 00:05:50.209
The following year, Cabot sailed west

00:05:50.211 --> 00:05:53.778
again with five ships, and then largely

00:05:54.158 --> 00:05:56.135
disappears from the historical record.

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:59.445
What happened to him remains one of the

00:05:59.447 --> 00:06:02.002
great mysteries of the age of exploration.

00:06:02.905 --> 00:06:06.225
But the door he had opened stayed open.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.604
Fast forward just over a century,

00:06:09.606 --> 00:06:12.111
and England began planting colonies

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:14.753
on the other side of the Atlantic:

00:06:14.755 --> 00:06:17.484
Virginia, then Barbados, then Jamaica.

00:06:18.339 --> 00:06:20.483
Virginia grew tobacco, and the

00:06:20.485 --> 00:06:22.556
Caribbean islands grew sugar.

00:06:23.538 --> 00:06:25.925
And Bristol, facing west, was

00:06:25.927 --> 00:06:28.888
perfectly placed to trade with them.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.958
There was one part of the Atlantic trade,

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:35.987
though, that Bristol’s merchants were not

00:06:36.133 --> 00:06:40.061
allowed to touch: the trade in slaves.

00:06:40.913 --> 00:06:43.835
All English trade with West Africa was

00:06:43.837 --> 00:06:46.681
legally controlled by a single London

00:06:46.682 --> 00:06:49.495
company, the Royal African Company.

00:06:50.662 --> 00:06:54.840
Then, in 1698, that changed.

00:06:55.571 --> 00:06:58.311
The monopoly ended, and merchants in

00:06:58.313 --> 00:07:00.974
other cities were free to enter the

00:07:00.974 --> 00:07:03.596
business for themselves, the ugly

00:07:03.598 --> 00:07:06.456
business of trading in human beings.

00:07:07.732 --> 00:07:10.616
Bristol's merchants moved fast.

00:07:11.532 --> 00:07:14.702
Their ships sailed in a great triangle.

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:17.811
First, south to West Africa,

00:07:17.813 --> 00:07:20.958
loaded with brass goods, guns and

00:07:20.989 --> 00:07:23.991
alcohol, to be exchanged for captive

00:07:23.993 --> 00:07:26.659
African men, women and children.

00:07:27.541 --> 00:07:29.979
Then came the second side of the

00:07:29.981 --> 00:07:32.417
triangle, the Atlantic crossing.

00:07:33.267 --> 00:07:36.076
Hundreds of enslaved people were packed

00:07:36.078 --> 00:07:38.524
below deck, chained together, with

00:07:38.535 --> 00:07:40.968
little room to move, little air, and

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:43.334
disease spreading through the hold.

00:07:44.411 --> 00:07:46.174
As you will know, many did

00:07:46.176 --> 00:07:47.802
not survive the journey.

00:07:48.638 --> 00:07:50.712
Those who did were sold in the

00:07:50.714 --> 00:07:53.201
Caribbean and the American colonies.

00:07:54.303 --> 00:07:57.113
And the ships came home to Bristol

00:07:57.115 --> 00:08:00.170
loaded with what this enslaved labour

00:08:00.193 --> 00:08:04.732
produced: sugar, tobacco, rum and cocoa.

00:08:05.982 --> 00:08:09.761
Over roughly a century, ships from Bristol

00:08:09.763 --> 00:08:13.000
made more than two thousand of these

00:08:13.012 --> 00:08:16.437
voyages, and carried around half a million

00:08:16.439 --> 00:08:19.535
enslaved Africans across the Atlantic.

00:08:20.698 --> 00:08:23.867
By the late 1730s, Bristol had become

00:08:23.869 --> 00:08:26.780
the leading slave port in Britain.

00:08:28.113 --> 00:08:29.991
The enslaved people themselves

00:08:29.993 --> 00:08:31.807
almost never came to Bristol.

00:08:32.558 --> 00:08:35.622
A small number arrived as servants.

00:08:35.666 --> 00:08:37.705
But the vast majority never

00:08:37.707 --> 00:08:39.819
saw the city that sold them.

00:08:40.848 --> 00:08:44.069
What arrived in Bristol was sweeter:

00:08:44.071 --> 00:08:46.933
sugar for the city's refineries,

00:08:47.166 --> 00:08:49.394
tobacco for its factories, and

00:08:49.396 --> 00:08:51.697
cocoa for its chocolate makers.

00:08:52.975 --> 00:08:55.721
And the money went everywhere: to the

00:08:55.723 --> 00:08:58.392
shipbuilders and rope makers, to the

00:08:58.403 --> 00:09:01.162
bankers who lent against the voyages, the

00:09:01.164 --> 00:09:03.921
sugar refiners and the tobacco merchants.

00:09:05.118 --> 00:09:07.725
And this is where Edward Colston

00:09:07.727 --> 00:09:09.843
comes back into our story.

00:09:10.823 --> 00:09:14.610
Colston was born in Bristol in 1636, and

00:09:14.612 --> 00:09:18.491
made his fortune in London as a merchant.

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:22.149
For twelve years he was a senior

00:09:22.151 --> 00:09:25.153
official of the Royal African Company,

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.777
the company that had kept Bristol’s

00:09:27.779 --> 00:09:29.924
merchants out of the slave trade.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:34.306
During his years with the company,

00:09:34.308 --> 00:09:37.620
its ships carried tens of thousands of

00:09:37.653 --> 00:09:40.151
enslaved Africans across the Atlantic.

00:09:40.909 --> 00:09:43.955
Roughly one in five of them died at sea,

00:09:43.957 --> 00:09:47.000
and those who didn't die on the journey,

00:09:47.369 --> 00:09:49.564
well, their fate wasn't much better.

00:09:50.906 --> 00:09:55.338
When Colston died in 1721, he left much

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:59.316
of his fortune to Bristol: schools,

00:09:59.789 --> 00:10:02.352
almshouses, which are homes for the

00:10:02.354 --> 00:10:04.988
poor, and charities for the elderly.

00:10:06.367 --> 00:10:08.841
And Bristol, in return,

00:10:08.843 --> 00:10:11.961
effectively made him a saint.

00:10:12.656 --> 00:10:15.041
His name went onto streets,

00:10:15.043 --> 00:10:17.602
schools, office buildings and

00:10:17.603 --> 00:10:19.658
the city's grandest concert hall.

00:10:20.337 --> 00:10:23.031
There was even a sweet bun named

00:10:23.033 --> 00:10:25.556
after him, eaten every year by

00:10:25.568 --> 00:10:27.713
schoolchildren on Colston Day.

00:10:28.803 --> 00:10:31.458
A child in Bristol could go to a school

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.773
with his name on it, eat a Colston

00:10:33.782 --> 00:10:36.630
bun once a year, and grow up walking

00:10:36.632 --> 00:10:39.637
past his statue in the centre of town.

00:10:40.374 --> 00:10:43.086
He was the city's greatest selfless

00:10:43.088 --> 00:10:45.876
benefactor, a wonderful man, as long

00:10:45.877 --> 00:10:47.881
as you didn't think too much about

00:10:47.883 --> 00:10:49.709
where all this money came from.

00:10:50.974 --> 00:10:52.925
Not everyone in the city was

00:10:52.927 --> 00:10:55.154
prepared to keep schtum, though.

00:10:56.308 --> 00:11:00.212
In the summer of 1787, a young clergyman

00:11:00.214 --> 00:11:04.312
called Thomas Clarkson arrived in Bristol.

00:11:05.433 --> 00:11:08.134
He had decided to devote his life

00:11:08.136 --> 00:11:10.589
to ending the slave trade, and

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:13.044
he came to one of the great slave

00:11:13.046 --> 00:11:15.579
ports of the day to gather evidence.

00:11:17.012 --> 00:11:19.664
The ship owners wouldn't talk to

00:11:19.666 --> 00:11:22.731
him, so he went to the docks instead.

00:11:23.844 --> 00:11:26.720
In a small pub called the Seven Stars,

00:11:26.722 --> 00:11:29.671
down a back alley near the harbour, the

00:11:29.673 --> 00:11:32.254
landlord introduced him to the men who

00:11:32.256 --> 00:11:34.700
had actually worked the slave ships.

00:11:35.972 --> 00:11:38.642
Clarkson sat and listened, night

00:11:38.644 --> 00:11:41.228
after night, as ships' surgeons

00:11:41.228 --> 00:11:43.496
and ordinary sailors described

00:11:43.498 --> 00:11:45.386
what happened below deck.

00:11:46.654 --> 00:11:48.148
He wrote it all down, and

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:49.641
he took it to Parliament.

00:11:50.492 --> 00:11:52.765
It took twenty years of

00:11:52.767 --> 00:11:55.235
campaigning, but in 1807,

00:11:55.666 --> 00:11:58.365
Parliament outlawed the slave trade.

00:11:59.567 --> 00:12:02.072
Now, Bristol's own golden age as a

00:12:02.074 --> 00:12:04.504
port had actually started to fade

00:12:04.898 --> 00:12:07.525
as early as the 1740s, as Liverpool

00:12:07.527 --> 00:12:10.076
took over the slave trade and then

00:12:10.078 --> 00:12:11.768
the Atlantic trade in general.

00:12:12.951 --> 00:12:15.323
Liverpool had bigger docks and an

00:12:15.325 --> 00:12:17.838
easier approach from the sea, while

00:12:18.058 --> 00:12:20.153
Bristol's ships had to creep up

00:12:20.155 --> 00:12:22.383
a narrow, winding gorge with some

00:12:22.384 --> 00:12:24.295
of the highest tides in the world.

00:12:25.738 --> 00:12:28.128
This meant that the city had been

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:30.881
forced to fight back with engineering.

00:12:32.287 --> 00:12:35.195
In 1809 it completed its Floating

00:12:35.197 --> 00:12:38.632
Harbour, sealing off two miles of river

00:12:38.914 --> 00:12:41.555
behind lock gates so that ships in the

00:12:41.557 --> 00:12:43.988
heart of the city could stay afloat

00:12:44.148 --> 00:12:46.344
whatever the tide was doing outside.

00:12:47.409 --> 00:12:49.914
But probably the most famous feat

00:12:49.916 --> 00:12:52.571
of Bristol engineering is a bridge,

00:12:52.909 --> 00:12:55.240
the Clifton Suspension Bridge,

00:12:55.242 --> 00:12:57.494
and its designer, a man named

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:59.482
Isambard Kingdom Brunel, which

00:12:59.484 --> 00:13:01.458
is just a great name by the way.

00:13:02.744 --> 00:13:04.982
In 1831, as an unknown

00:13:04.984 --> 00:13:08.035
twenty-four-year-old, he won a

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:10.632
competition to design a bridge

00:13:10.634 --> 00:13:12.861
over Bristol's Avon Gorge.

00:13:13.384 --> 00:13:15.349
And he would become the most

00:13:15.351 --> 00:13:17.805
famous engineer in British history.

00:13:19.089 --> 00:13:21.579
Two years later, he was made chief

00:13:21.581 --> 00:13:23.703
engineer of the Great Western

00:13:23.704 --> 00:13:25.769
Railway, which was a railway line

00:13:25.771 --> 00:13:27.583
to connect London to Bristol.

00:13:28.789 --> 00:13:30.872
At an early board meeting, when

00:13:30.874 --> 00:13:33.223
someone worried the railway was too

00:13:33.224 --> 00:13:35.796
long, Brunel is said to have joked:

00:13:35.798 --> 00:13:38.148
why not make it longer, and have

00:13:38.148 --> 00:13:40.309
a steamboat go from Bristol to New

00:13:40.311 --> 00:13:42.597
York, and call it the Great Western?

00:13:43.741 --> 00:13:44.684
It was a joke.

00:13:45.447 --> 00:13:46.370
He then did it.

00:13:47.255 --> 00:13:49.771
The steamship Great Western, which was

00:13:49.773 --> 00:13:52.155
built in Bristol, entered service in

00:13:52.217 --> 00:13:55.668
1838 as the first steamship designed

00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:59.023
specifically to cross the Atlantic.

00:14:00.307 --> 00:14:02.987
Five years later, from a Bristol

00:14:02.989 --> 00:14:05.835
dock, Brunel launched the SS Great

00:14:05.858 --> 00:14:08.366
Britain, the largest ship in the

00:14:08.368 --> 00:14:11.344
world, and the first ocean liner built

00:14:11.344 --> 00:14:14.041
of iron and driven by a propeller.

00:14:15.247 --> 00:14:17.401
The bridge that started it

00:14:17.403 --> 00:14:19.886
all was finished in 1864, five

00:14:19.889 --> 00:14:21.680
years after Brunel's death,

00:14:21.682 --> 00:14:23.736
and you can still see it today.

00:14:24.461 --> 00:14:26.934
The SS Great Britain, too, is still

00:14:26.936 --> 00:14:29.336
in Bristol today, back in the very

00:14:29.347 --> 00:14:31.331
dry dock where she was built, a

00:14:31.333 --> 00:14:33.699
ten-minute stroll from the spot where

00:14:33.753 --> 00:14:36.519
Cabot's boat still sits on the water.

00:14:37.526 --> 00:14:40.201
Now, let's jump forward to the

00:14:40.203 --> 00:14:43.410
spring of 1963, as there is one more

00:14:43.431 --> 00:14:44.941
thing to tell you about Bristol.

00:14:45.786 --> 00:14:47.857
Bristol was doing well.

00:14:48.303 --> 00:14:51.134
The docks were still busy, the factories

00:14:51.136 --> 00:14:53.328
were hiring, and since the war,

00:14:53.548 --> 00:14:55.986
several thousand people had arrived

00:14:55.988 --> 00:14:58.493
from the Caribbean, from Jamaica and

00:14:58.495 --> 00:15:01.043
Barbados above all, invited to help

00:15:01.045 --> 00:15:03.372
rebuild a country they had grown

00:15:03.374 --> 00:15:05.160
up calling "the mother country".

00:15:06.374 --> 00:15:08.534
The city's buses were run by

00:15:08.536 --> 00:15:10.694
the Bristol Omnibus Company.

00:15:11.705 --> 00:15:14.486
And it had an unwritten rule, never

00:15:14.488 --> 00:15:17.028
formalised but well known in the

00:15:17.029 --> 00:15:20.146
city: it did not employ Black or

00:15:20.148 --> 00:15:23.848
Asian people as drivers or conductors.

00:15:24.677 --> 00:15:26.045
They could work in the

00:15:26.047 --> 00:15:27.725
canteens and the workshops.

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:30.469
They could not work on the buses.

00:15:31.808 --> 00:15:34.095
A youth worker called Paul

00:15:34.097 --> 00:15:36.822
Stephenson decided to prove it.

00:15:37.712 --> 00:15:40.734
He arranged a job interview for a young

00:15:40.736 --> 00:15:43.756
warehouseman called Guy Bailey, who had

00:15:43.787 --> 00:15:46.484
every qualification the company asked for.

00:15:47.439 --> 00:15:49.442
Then Stephenson telephoned the

00:15:49.444 --> 00:15:51.713
company and mentioned, in passing,

00:15:52.114 --> 00:15:54.547
that Mr Bailey was West Indian.

00:15:54.816 --> 00:15:55.533
He was Black.

00:15:56.616 --> 00:15:58.562
The interview, he was politely

00:15:58.564 --> 00:16:00.119
informed, was cancelled.

00:16:01.273 --> 00:16:03.553
Stephenson and a small group of

00:16:03.555 --> 00:16:05.760
fellow campaigners went to the

00:16:05.761 --> 00:16:08.680
newspapers, and on the 29th of

00:16:08.682 --> 00:16:12.183
April 1963 they announced a boycott.

00:16:13.416 --> 00:16:15.612
Bristol's Caribbean community, and

00:16:15.614 --> 00:16:17.809
a good many white Bristolians with

00:16:17.809 --> 00:16:20.004
them, stopped using the buses.

00:16:20.454 --> 00:16:21.656
They walked, they

00:16:21.658 --> 00:16:23.424
cycled, they shared cars.

00:16:24.537 --> 00:16:26.444
The story went national.

00:16:27.096 --> 00:16:29.334
It was raised in Parliament, and

00:16:29.336 --> 00:16:31.571
Harold Wilson, the leader of the

00:16:31.571 --> 00:16:33.780
opposition and soon to be Prime

00:16:33.782 --> 00:16:35.989
Minister, spoke out in support.

00:16:37.275 --> 00:16:39.974
On the 28th of August 1963, the

00:16:39.976 --> 00:16:43.369
company gave in and announced that they

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:45.595
would become what we would call today

00:16:45.597 --> 00:16:47.639
an "equal opportunities" employer.

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:52.153
Sure enough, three weeks later, a Sikh

00:16:52.155 --> 00:16:55.084
man called Raghbir Singh became the

00:16:55.084 --> 00:16:58.497
city's first non-white bus conductor.

00:16:59.668 --> 00:17:02.305
And two years after that, Parliament

00:17:02.307 --> 00:17:05.236
passed the Race Relations Act, the first

00:17:05.251 --> 00:17:08.491
British law against racial discrimination.

00:17:09.396 --> 00:17:11.455
The Bristol bus boycott was

00:17:11.457 --> 00:17:13.742
one of the reasons it existed.

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:17.580
And this brings us back to the bronze

00:17:17.582 --> 00:17:20.158
man at the bottom of the harbour.

00:17:21.448 --> 00:17:24.340
Fast forward to the late 20th century,

00:17:24.342 --> 00:17:26.851
and Bristol's Caribbean community

00:17:27.103 --> 00:17:29.120
was into its third generation.

00:17:29.994 --> 00:17:32.696
For many people in that community,

00:17:32.698 --> 00:17:35.399
the statue was a daily reminder of

00:17:35.399 --> 00:17:37.464
how the city had made its money.

00:17:38.876 --> 00:17:41.798
The statue of Edward Colston had been

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:44.563
put up in 1895, more than a hundred

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:47.468
and seventy years after his death, at a

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:50.419
time when Victorian Bristol wanted civic

00:17:50.427 --> 00:17:53.261
heroes and nobody asked too many questions

00:17:53.263 --> 00:17:55.556
about how they'd made their money.

00:17:56.741 --> 00:17:59.749
Throughout most of the twentieth century,

00:17:59.751 --> 00:18:02.317
Colston's presence was a non-issue.

00:18:02.883 --> 00:18:05.462
But from the 1990s onwards,

00:18:05.464 --> 00:18:08.518
historians, campaigners and many

00:18:08.607 --> 00:18:11.098
Black Bristolians became more vocal

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.518
in their questioning, asking why a

00:18:13.526 --> 00:18:16.158
city with a large Caribbean community

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:18.791
was still celebrating a slave trader.

00:18:20.105 --> 00:18:23.033
Paul Stephenson, who was by then in his

00:18:23.035 --> 00:18:25.960
sixties, was among the loudest of them.

00:18:26.957 --> 00:18:28.325
There were campaigns

00:18:28.327 --> 00:18:29.898
to rename Colston Hall.

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:32.923
There was a long, drawn-out attempt simply

00:18:32.925 --> 00:18:35.260
to add a second plaque to the statue,

00:18:35.571 --> 00:18:38.111
explaining his role in the slave trade.

00:18:38.865 --> 00:18:41.106
It collapsed in 2019, when the

00:18:41.108 --> 00:18:43.720
city couldn't agree on the wording.

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:47.741
Then came June 2020, the ropes,

00:18:47.743 --> 00:18:50.433
the splash, and the pictures

00:18:50.617 --> 00:18:52.132
that went around the world.

00:18:53.535 --> 00:18:55.438
The statue was fished out of

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:57.341
the harbour four days later.

00:18:58.685 --> 00:19:01.014
In September 2020, the concert

00:19:01.016 --> 00:19:03.499
hall dropped Colston's name, and

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:05.360
it became the Bristol Beacon.

00:19:06.691 --> 00:19:09.852
Four protesters were put on trial for

00:19:09.854 --> 00:19:13.013
criminal damage, and in January 2022,

00:19:13.399 --> 00:19:16.898
a jury found all four not guilty.

00:19:18.127 --> 00:19:20.565
Bristol's famous street artist,

00:19:20.567 --> 00:19:22.924
Banksy, had a suggestion about

00:19:22.925 --> 00:19:24.655
what to do with the statue.

00:19:25.181 --> 00:19:27.939
Put it back on its pedestal, he said,

00:19:27.941 --> 00:19:30.996
and add bronze figures of the protesters,

00:19:31.372 --> 00:19:33.550
caught in the act of pulling it down.

00:19:34.018 --> 00:19:36.095
That way, everyone would be happy.

00:19:37.006 --> 00:19:38.928
The city hasn't taken him up on it.

00:19:40.104 --> 00:19:43.120
The statue itself now lies in a Bristol

00:19:43.122 --> 00:19:45.672
museum, horizontal, still dented,

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:48.699
still covered in spray paint, next to

00:19:48.701 --> 00:19:51.309
the placards from that day in 2020.

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:54.193
And the shockwave travelled

00:19:54.195 --> 00:19:55.610
far beyond Bristol.

00:19:56.140 --> 00:19:59.008
Within weeks, towns and institutions

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:02.036
across Britain were re-examining their

00:20:02.037 --> 00:20:04.747
own statues, street names and buildings.

00:20:05.826 --> 00:20:09.212
This, as you might imagine, is still a hot

00:20:09.214 --> 00:20:12.437
potato, and political and public opinion

00:20:12.459 --> 00:20:15.409
is divided over how modern Britain should

00:20:15.411 --> 00:20:18.358
deal with its colonial past, and with the

00:20:18.358 --> 00:20:21.036
legacies of people like Edward Colston.

00:20:22.342 --> 00:20:24.461
The reality is that Bristol

00:20:24.463 --> 00:20:26.893
wouldn't be Bristol without its

00:20:26.901 --> 00:20:29.966
past, but the uncomfortable reality

00:20:29.968 --> 00:20:32.506
is that a lot of that past is

00:20:32.508 --> 00:20:34.438
not very pleasant to think about.

00:20:36.012 --> 00:20:38.684
OK, then, that is it for today's episode

00:20:38.686 --> 00:20:41.022
on Bristol, and with that comes the

00:20:41.036 --> 00:20:43.165
end of this three-part mini-series on

00:20:43.167 --> 00:20:45.466
three cities that shaped modern Britain.

00:20:45.914 --> 00:20:47.224
I wonder, have you been

00:20:47.226 --> 00:20:48.533
to any of these cities?

00:20:48.803 --> 00:20:49.383
All of them?

00:20:49.915 --> 00:20:51.251
In any case, I hope you found

00:20:51.253 --> 00:20:52.357
it interesting, and that

00:20:52.357 --> 00:20:53.586
you've learnt something new.

00:20:53.952 --> 00:20:55.994
If you'd like to keep going with some

00:20:55.996 --> 00:20:57.981
of these themes, we have episodes on

00:20:57.981 --> 00:21:00.886
the Transatlantic Slave Trade, which is

00:21:00.888 --> 00:21:04.015
episode number 447, on the British Empire,

00:21:04.039 --> 00:21:06.883
which is episode number 109, and even on

00:21:06.885 --> 00:21:09.728
the rise and fall of Concorde, which was

00:21:09.729 --> 00:21:13.433
built in Bristol, which is episode 243.

00:21:14.223 --> 00:21:16.811
You've been listening to English Learning

00:21:16.813 --> 00:21:19.210
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:21:19.479 --> 00:21:22.141
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:21:22.143 --> 00:21:24.592
I'll catch you in the next episode.
